{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-125112",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2))(x+2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{1-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-2)=-\\frac{1}{(1-(-2))^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(-2)}{2} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{9})}{2}=-\\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
